Subject: [#94-9-277] #2(3) Extropians Digest #...

Ulysses discovers the mysteries of the sun's south pole.

The pass currently being carried out by ESA's probe Ulysses* has so far been
a total success and had already yielded a first clutch of surprise results
concerning this unexplored region.
scientists at ESA's space research and technology center in Noordwick (The
Netherlands) this morning (Sept.16th 1994) gave a very positive account of
the South polar phase, which started in June and will be completed in
November. In particular they concluded that the south polar region displayed
unexpected magnetic and dynamic characteristics; this probably means that the
thinking on the magnetic structure of the Sun will have to be revised.
The first thing that surprised the scientists was the low cosmic radiation
activity above the south pole and the remarkable absence of a south magnetic
pole.
Could it be that the Sun has no south magnetic pole ?
Or is one suddenly going to appear before Ulysses completes its pass ?
The structure of the solar magnetic field in the southern polar region is not
as predicted by the models. The instruments on board Ulysses
have detected a new type of very slowly varying electromagnetic wavs , with
oscillation periods of 10 to 20 hours. The experts' theory is that this is
due to an unexpected phenomenon that conveys the solar magnetic field into
space through the solar wind.
Just reporting my gleanings !
