From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 19 Sep 1994 12:59:24 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-284] Omega Point

Nice article Mike (Price).

Tipler does indeed assumes one of his conclusions in his boundary
condition.  In he were right, and if we were sure we were the only in
life our universe, and that no other life would evolve if we were
gone, then if we had a bomb that could destroy us all, we could do
amazing things.  Set up a device so that either the bomb goes off, or
something wonderful, unlikely but physically possible, happens.  Then
the universe has no choice - something wonderful must happen!  Yeah,
right.

And I find little comfort in the scenario that all possible (finite)
experiences might one day be simulated.  If it doesn't matter how
times and places a given experience is "run", then no choices now
would matter, at least if my values are experience-based - all
experiences would happen at least once.  If it does matter how many
times and places, then I very much care what the future simulations
are focused on, and find little comfort in seeing that everything,
wonderful and horrible, is run at least once. 

Robin Hanson

